#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""bgw - command line client for the Browser Gateway daemon.

Python standard library only, so it runs anywhere an agent can run a shell
command (including sandboxes without Node).

  bgw fetch <url>      fetch a page through the operator's browser
  bgw search <query>   search the web through the operator's browser
  bgw read <page>      continue reading a stored page
  bgw pages <query>    search the stored pages
  bgw export <id>      write the stored content of a page, or the bytes of a retrieved
                       file (fl_...), exactly as stored, to a file. A retrieved file
                       goes into the export directory under a name made here
                       (<file id> and an ending by its kind) unless -o names a path.
                       A page (pg_...) is for the operator: its content comes without
                       markers, and the daemon hands it out for admin.token only
  bgw status           is the gateway paired, connected, paused
  bgw doctor           check the whole chain and say what is wrong
  bgw pair             one-time code for the browser extension (operator)
  bgw pending          pages with a human check that wait for you (operator)
  bgw open <job>       bring one of those pages up in the browser (operator)
  bgw debug-job <url>  raw job result as JSON (operator, debugging)
  bgw mcp              MCP server on stdin/stdout for an agent's client (Claude Code):
                       the gateway's tools, with the proof step before every request,
                       and file_export, which writes a retrieved file into the export
                       directory, under a name made here
  bgw mcp-config       print how to register "bgw mcp" in Claude Code (changes nothing)
  bgw daemon <cmd>     start | stop | status of a daemon run from this checkout (operator)

Exit codes:
  0  ok
  2  the gateway answered, and the answer is an error (a page that could not
     be fetched, a job that did not end ok, a doctor check that failed)
  1  no answer worth having: the daemon is not running or is not the daemon,
     a token is missing, the command line is wrong

The daemon has to prove that it holds the token before the token is sent to
it: whatever listens on the port gets a random nonce and must answer with an
HMAC under the token over that nonce and the port. A process that merely sits
on the port cannot, and gets nothing - not even by passing the nonce on to a
daemon that listens somewhere else.

Starting on demand: where the operator switched it on (the file "on-demand" in
the config directory, written by tools/install.sh on-demand), fetch, search,
read, pages, export, pair, pending and open, and a tool call through "mcp",
start the daemon as "bgw daemon start" does when nothing listens on the port.
The proof step comes after that, as always. After such a start fetch, search
and open give the browser extension up to 45 seconds to connect. The daemon is
not stopped again when idle.

Environment:
  BGW_URL               daemon address, default http://127.0.0.1:8791.
                        Only http://127.0.0.1:<port> is accepted.
  BGW_TOKEN_FILE        agent token file, default ~/.config/browser-gateway/client.token
  BGW_ADMIN_TOKEN_FILE  operator token file, used by "pair", "pending", "open",
                        "debug-job", "status --admin", "export" of a page and,
                        when it can be read, "doctor"; default
                        ~/.config/browser-gateway/admin.token
  BGW_DOWNLOAD_DIR      the export directory: where file_export of "mcp" and "export"
                        of a file without -o write. Default
                        ~/.local/share/browser-gateway/exports (made on demand, 0700):
                        on purpose not below the working directory of whoever runs this.
                        It must be owned by you and not writable by others. A file that is
                        there already (same size and SHA-256) is not written twice, and the
                        directory holds at most 4096 MiB of exports without -o
  BGW_CONFIG_DIR        the daemon's config directory, default
  BGW_DATA_DIR          ~/.config/browser-gateway, and its data directory, default
                        ~/.local/share/browser-gateway (pid file and log go there).
                        Read by "daemon" and by a start on demand; the switch for
                        that start is looked for in the config directory
  BGW_ON_DEMAND         1: start the daemon on demand in this call, whether the
                        switch is there or not; 0: never in this call

On Windows the defaults are below %LOCALAPPDATA%\\browser-gateway, as the daemon
has them: config\\ (client.token, admin.token, the switch), data\\ (pid file and
log) and data\\exports. LOCALAPPDATA must then be an absolute path (C:\\... or a
share); where it is not, only the BGW_* variables above say where things are. Owner and mode bits
are not checked there: the ACL of the user profile keeps others out. Links and
junctions in place of a token file or of the export directory are refused.
"""

import argparse
import base64
